.TH RENDEZVOUS 2
.SH NAME
rendezvous \- user level process synchronization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "rendezvous :: proc(tag: u64, value: u64) -> (partner: u64, ok: bool)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rendezvous
lets two processes meet at a common
.I tag
and exchange a value.
Together with shared memory (see
.IR fork (2)
and
.IR segattach (2)),
it lets cooperating processes arrange their own scheduling.
.PP
The first process to call
.I rendezvous
with a given
.I tag
sleeps.
When a second calls it with the same
.IR tag ,
the two
.I value
arguments are exchanged: each call answers the other's
.IR value ,
with
.B ok
true, and both processes wake.
.PP
The set of tags two processes may meet on is their
.I "rendezvous group" ,
inherited across a fork unless
.B RFREND
was set (see
.IR fork (2)),
which starts the caller in a group of its own.
.PP
.I Ok
is false when a note interrupted the wait; in that case
.I partner
is zero, which a program should therefore avoid using as an ordinary
value.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/user.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR segattach (2),
.IR semacquire (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Ok
is false on a note; there is no other failure.
.SH BUGS
At most sixty-four rendezvous are pending across the machine at once.
